\section{Dual-Mode Josephson Bias Mapping}
\label{sec:bias-mapping}

Once the conditioned strain register has been computed, the Quantum Logic Engine
executes a deterministic bias-selection rule to stabilize the Josephson
rectification lattice. The RTL core implements a two-path mapping strategy:

\begin{equation}
    S_{\text{upper}} = S_{\text{cond}}[63:32], \qquad
    S_{\text{lower}} = S_{\text{cond}}[31:0].
\end{equation}

High-energy transient compensation is triggered when the upper half-word exceeds
the signed threshold:

\begin{equation}
    S_{\text{upper}} > 0x7\text{FFFFFFF}.
\end{equation}

In this regime, the bias output is computed via a bitwise OR operation:

\begin{equation}
    V_{\text{bias}}^{(\text{transient})}
        = T_{\text{harvest}} \;\vert\; S_{\text{lower}}.
\end{equation}

For all other strain conditions, the system enters continuous impedance-tracking
mode, applying a bitwise XOR:

\begin{equation}
    V_{\text{bias}}^{(\text{continuous})}
        = T_{\text{harvest}} \;\oplus\; S_{\text{lower}}.
\end{equation}

This dual-mode mapping ensures that the Josephson junction grid remains stable
under both smooth and discontinuous strain-field perturbations.

\section{Femtosecond Nonce Synchronization}
\label{sec:nonce-sync}

The internal nonce counter advances deterministically at each femtosecond clock
edge:

\begin{equation}
    N(t + \Delta t_{\text{femto}}) = N(t) + 1,
\end{equation}

where $\Delta t_{\text{femto}} = 10^{-15}\,\text{s}$. This guarantees that every
metric strain sample is cryptographically isolated from its predecessor, even
under high-frequency structural oscillations. Because the nonce is never exposed
outside the RTL enclave, the conditioned strain register cannot be reconstructed
by any external observer.

\section{XOR-Shard Conditioning Layer}
\label{sec:xor-conditioning}

Before bias mapping, the raw strain vector is passed through the XOR-shard
conditioning layer:

\begin{equation}
    S_{\text{cond}} = S_{\text{raw}} \oplus N(t).
\end{equation}

This operation enforces non-replayability and hardware-level obfuscation. Even
if two consecutive strain readings are identical, the conditioned output will
differ due to the advancing nonce. This prevents temporal aliasing across
distributed simulation nodes and eliminates the possibility of deterministic
strain-pattern injection attacks.

\section{Secure Reset and Fault Containment}
\label{sec:fault-containment}

If the authorization key fails verification, the RTL core immediately asserts
the \texttt{security\_fault} flag. This triggers a hardware-level containment
sequence:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Zeroization of the internal nonce register.
    \item Zeroization of the conditioned strain register.
    \item Forced nulling of the Josephson bias output.
    \item Suspension of all downstream harvesting-grid updates.
\end{enumerate}

The containment sequence ensures that no corrupted or unauthorized metric data
propagates into the physical control lattice. Normal operation resumes only
after a valid cryptographic handshake is re-established.

\section{Deterministic Harvest-Line Stabilization}
\label{sec:harvest-stabilization}

The reference harvest-line constant,
\begin{equation}
    T_{\text{harvest}} = 0x00003228,
\end{equation}
corresponds to a calibrated inductive setting of $12.84\,\text{nH}$ within the
Layer~5 Josephson junction matrix. By combining this constant with the
conditioned strain register through OR/XOR operations, the RTL core maintains
stable DC extraction even under rapid strain-field fluctuations.

The stabilization rule is intentionally bitwise rather than arithmetic, ensuring
that the bias output remains bounded and monotonic across all operational
states. This prevents over-biasing events that could destabilize the
rectification grid or induce destructive resonance modes.